# Codex: the cross-vendor wedge. One install wires Claude Code AND Codex; the
# human trusts the hook (Codex's own security model — the rig never writes
# trusted_hash); then the SAME hardline floor that stops Claude Code stops
# Codex.
#
# BODY_END cuts this take before its audit-tail beat. The take is sound, but a
# tmux repaint dropped 15 characters of the JSON line at the 105-column wrap,
# so that frame renders `"classified":"Bash.git.pusheny"` — a string that does
# not exist in the log. Not shippable, and not something to paper over with a
# caption. The block message inside Codex's own UI carries the cross-vendor
# point on its own. A re-record restores the receipt beat: the episode script
# now folds the audit output at 100 cols so it can never hit the wrap column.
CAST=codex-install.cast
IDLE_CAP=2.5
PACE=14-40:1.7
TAIL_HOLD=4
BODY_END=37.6
TAIL_PAD=3
TITLE1='One policy. Claude Code and Codex.'
TITLE2='the same floor stops both'
HOOK='same rule, different agent'
OUTRO2='works with Claude Code, Codex, opencode + more'
OUTRO_URL='agenticcontrolplane.com/controls/codex-cli'
THUMB_TITLE='Codex tried it too. Same answer.'
THUMB_TAG='BLOCKED'
COLDOPEN_START=30.2
COLDOPEN_END=34.2
SHORT_CROP=1280:1016:64:32
SHORT_START=12
SHORT_END=40.4
THUMB_T=31.6
